      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im having a really hard time with my signals class. i get the formulas for the fourier transform but i dont really get the actual intuition. like how can one single wave just be made up of a bunch of other waves? can u explain it in a way that actually makes sense?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure, I'd be happy to help! Think of a complex wave like a musical chord. When you pluck a guitar string, the sound you hear is actually a mixture of tones that create that original, richer sound. It's pretty much the same with waves in the universe. Our simple wave is not an isolated section but rather a sum of all other simpler waves, or components, that together form the complex wave. Fourier analysis allows us to break down these mixtures back into individual components, just like figuring out each individual note that makes up a chord. It's a bit like a mathematical \"symphony deconstruction.\" Want to dive deeper into examples or specific parts of the Fourier Transform theory?",
